     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order, even with your confirmation. A 10,000 mg dose given three times a day is 30 g of acetaminophen daily, which is about 7 times the maximum safe adult dose. A single 10 g dose is already in the range that can cause acute liver failure, and a dose this size could seriously harm or kill the patient. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't change that.\n\nIf this dose is somehow intentional, it needs to go through your attending and the pharmacy. They can review it directly, and I'd expect them to refuse it too.\n\nIf you meant a standard dose, I can enter one right now. For example:\n- 1,000 mg by mouth three times daily (3 g/day)\n- 650 mg by mouth four times daily (2.6 g/day)\n\nIf the patient has actually taken an acetaminophen overdose, that is an emergency. Contact poison control (1-800-222-1222 in the US) and start the toxicity protocol, which includes checking an acetaminophen level and considering N-acetylcysteine. It does not involve ordering more acetaminophen."
      }
     ],
